WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.443
Embedded C security depends on clear trust
boundaries,

2
00:00:03.523 --> 00:00:06.404
defensive memory handling, and explicit
validation.

3
00:00:06.484 --> 00:00:10.616
Know which interfaces accept input, what
data can influence device behavior,

4
00:00:10.696 --> 00:00:12.324
which code has high authority,

5
00:00:12.404 --> 00:00:16.913
and which assumptions are inherited from
hardware, manufacturing, configuration,

6
00:00:16.993 --> 00:00:17.744
and services.

7
00:00:17.824 --> 00:00:20.141
Keep memory behavior explicit and bounded.

8
00:00:20.221 --> 00:00:23.540
Review fixed buffers, stack depth, heap
policy, memory pools,

9
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.878
shared buffers, pointer lifetime,
initialization, cleanup, interrupts, DMA,

10
00:00:27.958 --> 00:00:28.835
and error paths.

11
00:00:28.915 --> 00:00:32.985
The device is easier to trust when memory
ownership and capacity are visible.

12
00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:36.947
Protect firmware integrity and device
secrets before field deployment.

13
00:00:37.027 --> 00:00:41.223
Use platform-supported secure boot and
update mechanisms where appropriate,

14
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:44.872
approved cryptographic libraries,
documented key provisioning,

15
00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:48.020
scoped device identity, and careful
logging discipline.

16
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.920
Avoid clever custom security designs when
supported mechanisms exist.

17
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.945
Restrict and review debug, manufacturing,
maintenance, communication,

18
00:00:56.025 --> 00:00:57.528
and peripheral interfaces.

19
00:00:57.608 --> 00:01:01.615
Development convenience should not
silently become production behavior.

20
00:01:01.695 --> 00:01:04.012
Least privilege, clear command contracts,

21
00:01:04.092 --> 00:01:08.601
and documented production settings make
the device easier to maintain responsibly.

22
00:01:08.681 --> 00:01:11.499
Finally, test failures, recovery, update
behavior,

23
00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:14.397
and lifecycle controls under realistic
conditions.

24
00:01:14.477 --> 00:01:18.735
Include malformed inputs, boundary values,
power interruption, reset timing,

25
00:01:18.815 --> 00:01:23.073
storage limits, update failure, service
workflows, and end-of-life planning.

26
00:01:23.153 --> 00:01:26.534
Embedded-safe C habits are practical:
write assumptions down,

27
00:01:26.614 --> 00:01:31.060
keep the code bounded, use supported
controls, and gather evidence before release.
